{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.ZMod.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.GroupTheory.OrderOfElement","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.clearable_of_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.clearable_of_even","kind":"theorem","line":131,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/EpsMObstruction.lean#L131-L138","doc":"**Clearability.** An even obstruction can always be cancelled by an\neven shift — the last step of the representative-change argument, which\nuses only shifts realised by the defining five-term relation (all of\nwhich are even). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.inversion_even_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.inversion_even_iff","kind":"theorem","line":121,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/EpsMObstruction.lean#L121-L129","doc":"**The inversion contribution is even exactly when `a` is.** This is\nwhy the parity of the obstruction form is *not* preserved by the derived\ninversion relation, and hence why the parity statement is confined to the\ndefining five-term relation: inversion holds in the Bloch group only\nmodulo torsion, and `ZMod 24` sees precisely that torsion. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.inversion_closed_form","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.inversion_closed_form","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/EpsMObstruction.lean#L109-L119","doc":"**The inversion relation's closed form.** With `e(1/x) = -a` and\n`e(1 - 1/x) = 12 + b - a` (the second from `1 - 1/x = -(1-x)/x` together\nwith `e(-1) = 12`), the pair `[x] + [1/x]` contributes exactly\n`a ^ 2 - 12 * a`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.reflection_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.reflection_even","kind":"theorem","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/EpsMObstruction.lean#L101-L107","doc":"**The reflection relation is even.** `[x] + [1 - x]` contributes\n`2 * (a * b)`, where `a = e(x)` and `b = e(1 - x)`: the two symbols\ncarry the same pair of logarithms in opposite order. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.qSymbol_rescale","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.qSymbol_rescale","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/EpsMObstruction.lean#L92-L99","doc":"**Generator independence.** Rescaling the discrete logarithm by a\nunit `u` (i.e. choosing a different generator of `μ₂₄`) leaves the\nobstruction form unchanged, because `u ^ 2 = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.unit_sq_eq_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.unit_sq_eq_one","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/EpsMObstruction.lean#L83-L90","doc":"Every unit of `ZMod 24` squares to `1` — the group of units is\nelementary abelian. This is what makes `qSymbol` independent of which\ngenerator is used for the discrete logarithm. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.qSymbol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.qSymbol","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/EpsMObstruction.lean#L78-L81","doc":"The obstruction contribution of one Bloch symbol `[w]`: the product\nof the Teichmüller discrete logarithms of `w` and of `1 - w`, taken in\n`ZMod 24` (the base Teichmüller group at the prime 5 is `μ₂₄`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.orderOf_shift_factor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.orderOf_shift_factor","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/EpsMObstruction.lean#L65-L74","doc":"The shift factor `ζ ^ 24` has order exactly `m` when `ζ` has order\n`24 * m` — so the shift really does land in `μ_m`, which is what makes\nthe shift exponent well defined mod `m`. (No `m ≠ 0` hypothesis is\nneeded: the infinite-order case `m = 0` holds too.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.frobenius_shift","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Machinery.EpsMObstruction.frobenius_shift","kind":"theorem","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Machinery/EpsMObstruction.lean#L52-L63","doc":"**The shift law.** With σ acting by `g ↦ g ^ 25` (the Galois\ngenerator φ₅² on roots of unity at the prime 5), an element written as\n`ζ ^ e` is moved by exactly `(ζ ^ 24) ^ e`:\n\n    σ(ζ ^ e) = (ζ ^ 24) ^ e * (ζ ^ e).\n\nGeneric `CommGroup`; no arithmetic hypotheses are needed, the identity\nis pure exponent bookkeeping. ","signatureChunk":0}]}